data.content:
Setting `SafeAreaEdges="None"` on the page prevents the page from adding padding for safe areas, but there are two other things you likely need:

**1. On iOS**, set the platform-specific `IgnoreSafeArea` on the view itself:

```xml
<ContentPage xmlns:ios="clr-namespace:Microsoft.Maui.Controls.PlatformConfiguration.iOSSpecific;assembly=Microsoft.Maui.Controls"
             SafeAreaEdges="None">
    <Image Source="photo.jpg"
           Aspect="AspectFill"
           ios:VisualElement.IgnoreSafeArea="True" />
</ContentPage>
```

**2. On Android**, the app doesn't draw behind system bars by default. Enable edge-to-edge in your `MainActivity.cs`:

```csharp
protected override void OnCreate(Bundle? savedInstanceState)
{
    base.OnCreate(savedInstanceState);

    if (Window != null)
    {
        AndroidX.Core.View.WindowCompat.SetDecorFitsSystemWindows(Window, false);
        Window.SetStatusBarColor(Android.Graphics.Color.Transparent);
        Window.SetNavigationBarColor(Android.Graphics.Color.Transparent);
    }
}
```

**Key points:**
- `SafeAreaEdges="None"` — stops the *page* from adding inset padding
- `ios:VisualElement.IgnoreSafeArea="True"` — lets the *view* extend into the safe area on iOS
- `SetDecorFitsSystemWindows(false)` — lets content draw behind system bars on Android
- `Aspect="AspectFill"` — ensures the image actually fills the available space
